proofs.proof_tex:
Write the thin singular-value decomposition as
\[
L_n=U_nS_nV_n^{\top}.
\]
Assumption \(\operatorname{rank}(L_n)=r\) makes \(S_n\) invertible. For a fixed chart \(b\),
\[
L_{n,C_bP_b}=U_{n,C_b}S_nV_{n,P_b}^{\top}.
\]
The displayed rank hypothesis is \(r\). Since both outer factors have \(r\) columns, it implies
\(\operatorname{rank}(U_{n,C_b})=\operatorname{rank}(V_{n,P_b})=r\). Thus the two Gram matrices are invertible. The Moore--Penrose inverse of the displayed full-rank factorization is
\[
L_{n,C_bP_b}^{\dagger}
=V_{n,P_b}(V_{n,P_b}^{\top}V_{n,P_b})^{-1}
S_n^{-1}(U_{n,C_b}^{\top}U_{n,C_b})^{-1}U_{n,C_b}^{\top}.
\]
Direct multiplication, using each inverse only after the preceding rank conclusion, gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
L_{n,I_bP_b}L_{n,C_bP_b}^{\dagger}L_{n,C_bQ_b}
&=U_{n,I_b}S_nV_{n,P_b}^{\top}
V_{n,P_b}(V_{n,P_b}^{\top}V_{n,P_b})^{-1}
S_n^{-1}(U_{n,C_b}^{\top}U_{n,C_b})^{-1}U_{n,C_b}^{\top}
U_{n,C_b}S_nV_{n,Q_b}^{\top}\\
&=U_{n,I_b}S_nV_{n,Q_b}^{\top}
=L_{n,I_bQ_b}.
\end{aligned}
\]
By the four-block cover, \(I_b\times P_b\), \(C_b\times P_b\), and \(C_b\times Q_b\) are subsets of \(\Omega_n\). Assumption \(P_{\Omega_n}D_n=0\) and Definition \ref{def:observed-experiment} say that the mean of each of these observed blocks under \(\mathbb Q_{\vartheta,n}\) is the corresponding block of \(L_n\). Hence the right-hand side of the identity is a functional of the tuple-indexed observed mean, whereas the causal counterfactual remains \(L_{n,I_bQ_b}\); the identity proves their equality rather than defining it.
